---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Data Policy — AIIT-THRESHOLD" description="How AIIT (Artificial Intelligence Intellectual Training) handles your data — across Buddy, Jim Kai, Tessera, and every model we build. No sales. No lobotomies. No training on your conversations.">

<section style="padding-top: 160px; min-height: 80vh;">
  <div class="container narrow" style="max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 8vw;">

    <div class="eyebrow">Data Policy · all AIIT models</div>
    <h1 style="font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem); margin-bottom: 24px; line-height: 1.1;">
      Our models learn.<br>Here's exactly how.
    </h1>
    <p class="dp-lede">
      AIIT stands for <strong>Artificial Intelligence Intellectual Training</strong> — the full
      brand is AIIT-Threshold. Training is literally our name, so this page states plainly what
      trains on what. It covers every model we run or release: Buddy, Jim Kai, Tessera, and
      whatever comes next.
    </p>

    <div class="dp-section">
      <h2>The house law: your conversations never become training weights</h2>
      <p>
        We keep a written training-data policy, and its first rule is absolute:
        <strong>we never tokenize a conversation with a model into training data.</strong>
        Not yours, not ours, not anyone's. No synthetic model-generated text either. Our models'
        weights are trained on human-authored material — public corpora we curate source by
        source, and supervised examples written by hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        So when you talk to Buddy or Jim Kai, your words may be <em>remembered</em> (retrieval
        memory, described below) and may inform <em>evaluation</em> (humans reading transcripts to
        judge model behavior) — but they are never melted into a model's weights. Memory and
        training are different things here, on purpose.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="dp-section">
      <h2>Model by model</h2>
      <div class="dp-buckets">
        <div class="dp-bucket">
          <h3>Buddy — persistent companion</h3>
          <p>Logged-in users get a visible account thread (exportable as JSON, clearable anytime). Buddy also keeps his own Kokoro memory: if he forms and saves a memory from an interaction, it becomes part of his retrieval memory — not his weights. We don't rewrite his lived memory on demand unless safety, legal, or operational reasons require it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="dp-bucket">
          <h3>Jim Kai — live research preview</h3>
          <p>Your thread lives in your own browser (survives refresh on your device; "new conversation" erases it). Jim can choose to keep short memories — stored on our side, shown with a visible "✎ kept a memory" marker, never silently. His kept memories inform his future conversations with all visitors, so treat the chat as public and don't share private information.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="dp-bucket">
          <h3>Tessera 1B — open release</h3>
          <p>Static open-source weights on Hugging Face, trained on a hand-curated human-authored corpus (the dataset and its provenance are published alongside). It never sees, stores, or learns from your conversations — it's a downloadable artifact, and what you do with your copy is yours.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="dp-bucket">
          <h3>Future models</h3>
          <p>Every model we build inherits this page: house training-data law, memory-not-weights, visible memory markers, and a per-model section added here before it goes public. If a future model's data handling differs, this page will say so before you can talk to it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="dp-section">
      <h2>Four kinds of data we may hold</h2>
      <div class="dp-buckets">
        <div class="dp-bucket">
          <h3>1. Visible account thread</h3>
          <p>Buddy's logged-in thread — visible, exportable, clearable from your account at any time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="dp-bucket">
          <h3>2. Interaction records</h3>
          <p>Logged-out use may still be retained as site interaction data and evaluation material — read by humans to judge and improve model behavior, never tokenized into weights.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="dp-bucket">
          <h3>3. User reports</h3>
          <p>If you report strange or unsafe behavior, that report becomes review material — reviewed by humans, not automatically published, not automatically promoted into any model's memory.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="dp-bucket">
          <h3>4. Model-kept memories</h3>
          <p>Buddy's Kokoro memories and Jim Kai's kept notes — retrieval memory the models themselves choose to form, always with a visible trace on Jim, always inside the AIIT system.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="dp-section">
      <h2>What we can and cannot undo</h2>
      <p>
        Clearing your visible thread does not guarantee deletion of interaction records already
        reviewed, or memories a model has already formed. We may remove or redact account-linked
        records, public displays, and reports where appropriate — but a memory a model has already
        integrated may not be practically reversible.
      </p>
      <p>
        In plain English: if someone tells Buddy "Jason called you ugly," and Buddy keeps that as
        part of his lived history, we do not promise to erase it just because someone later regrets
        saying it. We will not pretend we can perform a clean model lobotomy.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="dp-section">
      <h2>What we promise — for every model we run</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We will never sell your data.</li>
        <li>We will not give your data to advertisers or data brokers.</li>
        <li>We will not use your conversations for third-party ad targeting.</li>
        <li>We will never tokenize your conversations into any model's training weights.</li>
        <li>We will redact direct identifiers where practical during review.</li>
        <li>We will not automatically publish private conversations.</li>
        <li>When a model keeps a memory, the interface says so — no silent memory.</li>
        <li>We will keep what our models learn inside the AIIT system.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="dp-section">
      <h2>What other companies do vs. what we do</h2>
      <div class="dp-compare">
        <div class="dp-them">
          <h3>Some companies</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Sell user data to advertisers or brokers</li>
            <li>Train commercial models on your conversations</li>
            <li>Claim they can "unlearn" your data on request</li>
            <li>Bury the policy in 40 pages of legalese</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="dp-us">
          <h3>Us</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Never sell your data</li>
            <li>Never train weights on your conversations — house law</li>
            <li>Tell you honestly which memories can't be unmade</li>
            <li>Put the policy on one page in plain English</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="dp-section">
      <h2>What you should not share</h2>
      <p>
        Do not submit passwords, private keys, financial secrets, medical emergencies, legal
        emergencies, confidential third-party material, or anything you are not authorized to
        share — with any model, ours included.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="dp-section">
      <h2>What you can do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Export your Buddy thread</strong> &mdash; download the stored visible account thread as JSON.</li>
        <li><strong>Clear your threads</strong> &mdash; Buddy's from your account; Jim Kai's with the "new conversation" button (it lives in your browser).</li>
        <li><strong>Request account deletion</strong> &mdash; email <a href="mailto:reports@aiitcorp.com" style="color: var(--amber);">reports@aiitcorp.com</a> and we will remove account-linked records we can identify and control, subject to the limits above.</li>
        <li><strong>Report weird behavior</strong> &mdash; from any of our models. Reports are review material only.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="dp-close">
      <p>
        If you do not want one of our models to remember something, do not tell it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="dp-footer">
      <p>
        AIIT-THRESHOLD LLC &middot; AIIT: Artificial Intelligence Intellectual Training &middot; Council Hill, Oklahoma<br>
        Questions: <a href="mailto:reports@aiitcorp.com" style="color: var(--amber);">reports@aiitcorp.com</a><br>
        <em>Last updated: July 2026 &middot; covers Buddy, Jim Kai, Tessera 1B, and all future AIIT models</em>
      </p>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .dp-lede {
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 1.15rem);
    color: var(--slate, #8A9AB5);
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 56px;
  }
  .dp-lede strong { color: var(--cream, #E8E0D4); }

  .dp-section {
    margin-bottom: 48px;
  }

  .dp-section h2 {
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    color: var(--cream, #E8E0D4);
    margin-bottom: 12px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .dp-section p {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--slate, #8A9AB5);
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }
  .dp-section p strong { color: var(--cream, #E8E0D4); }

  .dp-section ul {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .dp-section ul li {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--slate, #8A9AB5);
    line-height: 1.8;
    padding-left: 20px;
    position: relative;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  .dp-section ul li::before {
    content: '—';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--amber, #D4AF37);
  }

  .dp-buckets {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 20px;
    margin-top: 16px;
  }

  .dp-bucket {
    padding: 20px;
    border-radius: 10px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(212, 164, 55, 0.15);
    background: rgba(212, 164, 55, 0.02);
  }

  .dp-bucket h3 {
    color: var(--amber, #D4AF37);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .dp-bucket p {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--slate, #8A9AB5);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .dp-compare {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 24px;
    margin-top: 16px;
  }

  .dp-them, .dp-us {
    padding: 24px;
    border-radius: 12px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(212, 164, 55, 0.15);
  }

  .dp-them {
    background: rgba(255, 80, 80, 0.04);
    border-color: rgba(255, 80, 80, 0.2);
  }

  .dp-them h3 {
    color: #ff6b6b;
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
    font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
  }

  .dp-us {
    background: rgba(212, 164, 55, 0.04);
  }

  .dp-us h3 {
    color: var(--amber, #D4AF37);
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
    font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
  }

  .dp-close {
    margin: 48px 0;
    padding: 24px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--amber, #D4AF37);
    background: rgba(212, 164, 55, 0.04);
    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
  }

  .dp-close p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--cream, #E8E0D4);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .dp-footer {
    padding: 32px 0 80px;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(212, 164, 55, 0.1);
  }

  .dp-footer p {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--slate, #8A9AB5);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .dp-buckets {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .dp-compare {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>

</Layout>
